course: biology
lesson: 159
title: The Congo rainforest: gorillas, bonobos, okapis and forest elephants
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: The Congo rainforest in the middle of Africa is the second biggest rainforest on Earth, a dark green world of gorillas, chimpanzees, bonobos and elephants that walk under the trees.
explain: A silverback gorilla beats his chest with cupped hands, and the popping drum sound tells rivals how big he is, often so a real fight is not needed.
explain: Cartoon gorillas like King Kong thump their chests with fists, roar and smash things; real gorillas are mostly calm plant-eaters, and chest-beating is a show, not an attack.
explain: The forest also hides strange animals: the okapi with zebra-striped legs is really a giraffe's cousin, and the pangolin, covered in scales, rolls into a ball that even a leopard cannot open.
fact: Male gorillas beat their chests with cupped hands to make a loud popping sound, and bigger gorillas make deeper sounds, so others can judge size by ear.
fact: Western lowland gorillas visit swampy forest clearings called bais to eat water plants rich in salt and minerals.
fact: Bonobos live only south of the Congo River and settle many quarrels by sharing, grooming and play; females stick together and are often in charge.
fact: Chimpanzees and bonobos split apart when the Congo River became a barrier they could not swim across, roughly one to two million years ago.
fact: The okapi lives only in the Congo forest and is the giraffe's closest living relative; its stripy legs break up its outline in the shadows.
fact: The okapi's long tongue can reach its own eyes and ears to clean them.
fact: African forest elephants are smaller than savanna elephants, with straighter tusks that point down, and they spread the seeds of many big trees.
fact: The pangolin is covered in hard overlapping scales made of keratin, like fingernails, has no teeth, and licks up ants and termites with a very long tongue.
fact: African grey parrots are famous copiers of sounds; wild ones copy their flock's calls, and pet ones copy words and household noises.
fact: A grey parrot called Alex learned to name colours, shapes and numbers in experiments with Irene Pepperberg.
fact: Chimpanzees strip leaves off sticks to fish for termites, young chimps learn by watching their mothers, and different chimp groups have different tool habits.
fact: Giant African land snails can grow shells longer than a child's hand and have thousands of tiny teeth on a ribbon called a radula.
fact: In the dark Congo forest leopards often hunt by day as well as at night; they hunt duikers, monkeys and even pangolins, and drag prey up trees to keep it from other hunters.
fact: Duikers are small forest antelopes whose name means diver in Afrikaans, because they plunge into thick bushes to escape.
exercise: Why does a silverback beat his chest? | To show rivals how big and strong he is, often avoiding a fight | Because his chest itches | To call his food | Because he is about to attack someone every single time he does it
exercise: How does a gorilla beat its chest? | With cupped hands, making a popping drum sound | With closed fists, making a heavy thud like a hammer on wood | With a stick like a drum | With its feet
exercise: How can gorillas judge another gorilla's size by ear? | Bigger gorillas make deeper chest-beat sounds | Bigger gorillas beat faster | Bigger gorillas beat quieter | Bigger gorillas never beat their chests, because they are too proud
exercise: Why do lowland gorillas wade into swampy clearings? | To eat water plants rich in salt and minerals | To catch fish | To cool down in the water after a long fight with another male | To hide from rain
exercise: How do bonobos settle many quarrels? | By sharing, grooming and play | By fighting until one runs away | By ignoring each other for days | By calling a gorilla to decide
exercise: Where do bonobos live? | Only south of the Congo River | All over Africa, in every forest | Only north of the great Congo River | In the rainforests of South-East Asia
exercise: How did chimpanzees and bonobos become separate kinds? | The Congo River split their ancestors into two groups | Bonobos moved up to the cold mountains, far away from the chimps | Chimps learned to swim away | Scientists bred them apart
exercise: What is the okapi's closest living relative? | The giraffe | The striped zebra | The wild horse | The antelope
exercise: How do the okapi's stripy legs help it? | They break up its outline in the forest shadows | They scare leopards away | They keep it cool | They help it run faster by making its legs look longer in the dark
exercise: What can the okapi do with its long tongue? | Clean its own eyes and ears | Catch flying insects | Climb trees | Smell water underground
exercise: How are forest elephants different from savanna elephants? | Smaller, with straighter tusks pointing down | Bigger, with long curly tusks that point up to the sky | They have no tusks at all | They have hairy coats
exercise: What does a pangolin do when a leopard sniffs it? | Rolls into a tight ball of hard scales | Climbs onto the leopard | Sprays a bad smell at the leopard, the way a skunk does | Runs faster than the leopard
exercise: What are a pangolin's scales made of? | Keratin, like fingernails | Bone, like a turtle's hard shell | Stone, like a smooth pebble | Wood, like the bark of a tree
exercise: Why does a grey parrot copy a creaking door? | Pet parrots copy household sounds the way wild ones copy their flock | It is scared of the door | It wants to open the door | It is calling for its food to come through the door from the kitchen
exercise: How does a chimpanzee catch termites? | It pokes a stripped stick into the mound and pulls out termites | It digs the mound open with its feet and licks up the termites | It waits for termites to fly | It blows the termites out
exercise: How do young chimps learn to use tools? | By watching their mothers for years | They know it from birth without practice | Other animals teach them | They never learn
exercise: Why does a leopard drag prey up a tree? | To keep it away from other hunters | To dry the meat out in the hot sunshine | To share it with the birds in the trees | Because it cannot eat on the ground
exercise: What does a duiker do when it is scared? | Dives into thick bushes | Climbs up the nearest tall tree | Stands still and stamps its feet | Jumps into the nearest river
test: King Kong roars, thumps his chest with his fists and smashes a city. What is true of real gorillas? | They are mostly calm plant-eaters; chest-beating with cupped hands is a show, not an attack | Gorillas smash anything they meet | Gorillas eat people | Gorillas cannot beat their chests at all; that is only in films
test: «bonobos are just chimps with a different name» What is right? | They are a separate kind that lives south of the Congo River and solves many quarrels peacefully | Yes, exactly the same animal | Bonobos are a kind of gorilla | Bonobos are small monkeys with tails that live up in the trees
test: «look, a zebra with a giraffe's head!» What animal is it? | An okapi, the giraffe's closest relative | A young zebra | A mix of a zebra and a giraffe, born on a farm | A striped antelope
test: Two gorillas and one okapi meet in a clearing. How many legs, not counting the gorillas' arms? | 8 | 10 | 12 | 6
test: Three forest elephants walk past. How many trunks and how many tusks, if each has two? | 3 trunks and 6 tusks | 6 trunks and 3 tusks | 3 trunks and 3 tusks | 3 trunks and 12 tusks
test: What would happen to many big trees if forest elephants disappeared? | Their seeds would be spread much less, so fewer new trees would grow | They would grow faster without elephants walking about and eating their fruit | Nothing would change | They would spread seeds by walking
test: «my mate's parrot says hello, so it understands english» What is the best answer? | Most parrots copy sounds, though Alex the grey parrot showed some can learn names, colours and numbers | Yes, every parrot can speak like a person and understands every word it says | No, parrots cannot copy sounds | Parrots only copy cats
test: Two chimp groups use tools in different ways. What does that show? | Chimp groups have their own traditions learned from others | One group is clever and the other is not | Tools grow differently in each area | The chimps are copying whatever the scientists show them to do with sticks
test: A pangolin has no teeth. How does it eat? | It licks up ants and termites with a very long tongue | It swallows fruit whole | It eats only soft young leaves from the tops of the trees | It drinks only water
test: A snail the size of a fist crawls over a log. How does it eat leaves with no jaws like ours? | It scrapes them with thousands of tiny teeth on its radula | It sucks them through a straw | It soaks them in water | It does not eat at all
test: «leopards only hunt at night, so the forest's safe at noon» What is right? | In the dark rainforest leopards often hunt by day too | Yes, they sleep all day | Leopards never live in forests | Leopards only ever hunt out on the open savanna, never among trees
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the chest-beating of the male gorilla. | It is a display communicating size and strength, frequently obviating actual combat | It invariably precedes an attack upon a human being | It is performed with closed fists in order to inflict injury upon the rival male | It is a symptom of illness
exam: Identify the principal circumstance that led to the separation of chimpanzees and bonobos into distinct species. | The formation of the Congo River as a barrier which their ancestors could not cross | The migration of the bonobo to the mountains of East Africa, far from the river | The deliberate breeding of the two forms by human beings | The extinction of the gorilla within the region
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the okapi? | It is the closest living relative of the giraffe and inhabits only the Congo forest | It is a hybrid of the zebra and the giraffe | It is a species of antelope inhabiting the open savanna of East and Southern Africa | It is a juvenile zebra of a forest variety
exam: Given that each of four forest elephants bears two tusks, determine the aggregate number of tusks. | Eight | Four | Twelve | Sixteen
